## Why Go Advocates Say "It's Fast Enough"

This is where the nuance matters. Go is **fast enough** when:

| Scenario | Verdict |
|---|---|
| Web API parsing 1 KB requests at 10K rps | **Go is fine**. Latency budget is ~100 ms; JSON parse is <1 ms. |
| Parsing 100s of MB telemetry files, seeking insights | **C++ wins**. minutes of processing savings matter. |
| Garbage-collected languages are acceptable and you value dev speed | **Go wins**. 1 hour of dev time > 20% parse speed. |

**The claim that Go "matches C++ speed" is false for compute-intensive, memory-intensive workloads.** It's true for I/O-bound, low-throughput, latency-insensitive services where developer velocity is the bottleneck. But for heavy data processing—where you're parsing megabytes of structured data in tight loops—C++'s manual memory management, arena allocation, SIMD, and zero-GC design are game-changers.

