### 1. C++ Implementation

We’ll use `simdjson` because it’s currently the fastest JSON parser available, leveraging SIMD instructions. Comparing Go’s standard library against C++’s naive `nlohmann/json` wouldn’t be fair.

#### `cpp/simd_json.cpp`
```cpp
// simd_json.cpp
#include <simdjson.hpp>
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <chrono>
#include <string>

// Structure to unmarshal into (mimicking Go struct)
struct Record {
    std::string id;
    std::string name;
    double value;
};

void parse_json(const std::string& filepath) {
    simdjson::dom::parser parser;
    simdjson::dom::element doc;

    // Load file
    auto error = parser.load(filepath).get(doc);
    if (error) {
        std::cerr << "Error: " << error.message() << std::endl;
        return;
    }

    // Measure parsing time
    auto start = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

    // Iterate through objects (assuming array of objects)
    int count = 0;
    for (simdjson::dom::object obj : doc) {
        std::string id;
        std::string name;
        double value;

        obj["id"].get(id).ignore_error();
        obj["name"].get(name).ignore_error();
        obj["value"].get(value).ignore_error();

        count++;
    }

    auto end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    auto duration = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(end - start);

    std::cout << "C++ (simdjson): Parsed " << count << " records in " 
              << duration.count() << " ms" << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    parse_json("benchmark.json");
    return 0;
}
```

#### `cpp/Makefile`
```makefile
simd_json: simd_json.cpp
	g++ -std=c++17 -O3 -march=native -I../vendor/simdjson/src -L../vendor/simdjson/lib -o simd_json simd_json.cpp -lsimdjson
```

