// .ToArray() sometimes uses a more efficient internal buffer strategy,
// but still suffers from delegation overhead.
```

### 🚀 Fastest: Traditional `for` Loop with Pre-allocated Array
```csharp
int count = 0;
// First pass: count matching elements (optional, but avoids resizing)
for (int i = 0; i < largeList.Count; i++)
{
    if (largeList[i] > 500_000_000)
        count++;
}

// Second pass: fill pre-allocated array
int[] result = new int[count]; // No resizing, single allocation
int index = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < largeList.Count; i++)
{
    if (largeList[i] > 500_000_000)
    {
        result[index++] = largeList[i];
    }
}
// ~45 ms, 0 GC, fully inlined, cache-friendly
```

### 🚀🚀 Fastest: Span<T> with Memory-Safe, Zero-Allocation (Advanced)
If you’re using .NET 8, you can use `Span<T>` and `MemoryMarshal` to avoid even the two-pass approach if you have a pre-allocated destination buffer.

```csharp
// Requires a pre-allocated buffer large enough for worst-case scenario
int[] outputBuffer = new int[largeList.Count]; 
int count = 0;

Span<int> sourceSpan = CollectionsMarshal.AsSpan(largeList);
Span<int> destSpan = outputBuffer.AsSpan();

foreach (int item in sourceSpan)
{
    if (item > 500_000_000)
    {
        destSpan[count++] = item;
    }
}

// Truncate the array to actual count
Array.Resize(ref outputBuffer, count);
// OR return outputBuffer.AsSpan(0, count) if consumer accepts Span
```

