### Optimization Tips for .NET 8

If you must use LINQ for readability but need performance, consider these alternatives:

#### 1. Pre-size the List (Huge Win)
If you can estimate the result size, you can significantly reduce LINQ’s memory allocation overhead. Unfortunately, `Where().ToList()` doesn’t expose a capacity constructor. You’d need to write a helper:

```csharp
public static List<T> ToListFast<T>(this IEnumerable<T> source, int estimatedCapacity)
{
    var list = new List<T>(estimatedCapacity);
    foreach (var item in source)
        list.Add(item);
    return list;
}
```

#### 2. Use Span<T> or ArrayPool<T> for Critical Paths
If you’re processing 10 million integers, you should avoid `List<T>` entirely. Use a pre-allocated `Span<T>` or `ArrayPool<T>` to avoid all GC pressure.

```csharp
// Zero-allocation, fastest possible approach
int[] buffer = ArrayPool<int>.Shared.Rent(source.Count);
int count = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < source.Count; i++)
{
    if (source[i] > 2_000_000)
        buffer[count++] = source[i];
}
int[] result = new int[count];
Array.Copy(buffer, result, count);
ArrayPool<int>.Shared.Return(buffer);
```

#### 3. Use Parallel LINQ (PLinq) for Embarrassingly Parallel Operations
If the filter is CPU-bound and the order doesn’t matter, `.AsParallel().Where().ToList()` can use all CPU cores. For 10 million items, this can bring the time down to ~50–60ms on an 8-core machine, potentially competing with a single-threaded `for` loop.

