# Matrix Multiplication: `-O2` vs `-O3` in GCC

## Direct Answer: **Use `-O3` for Compute-Heavy Numerical Loops**

For matrix multiplication — a classic **compute-bound**, **regular memory access**, **vectorization-friendly** workload — `-O3` almost always outperforms `-O2` by **15–40%** on modern x86_64 CPUs. This is one of the clearest cases where `-O3`'s aggressiveness pays off.

### Why `-O3` Wins Here

| Optimization Aspect | `-O2` | `-O3` | Impact on Matrix Mult |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Loop vectorization (SIMD)** | Conservative | Aggressive (`-ftree-vectorize`, wider loops) | **Huge**: auto-generates AVX2/AVX-512 or SSE |
| **Loop unrolling** | Light | Heavy | Reduces loop overhead, improves instruction-level parallelism |
| **Function inlining** | Moderate | Very aggressive | Enables cross-function optimization of helper kernels |
| **Scalar optimizations** | Good | Best | Better strength reduction, cheaper math transforms |
| **Branch prediction hints** | Basic | Enhanced | Less relevant for tight loops but still helpful |
| **Binary size** | Smaller | Larger (5–20%) | Secondary concern for local binaries |
| **Register pressure** | Lower | Higher | Can backfire on very wide loops, but GCC handles this well |

The key differentiator is **auto-vectorization**. `-O3` enables more aggressive vectorization decisions and often uses wider vectors (e.g., 256-bit AVX2 or 512-bit AVX-512 if available and compiled with `-march=native`). Matrix multiplication is essentially:

```
C[i][j] += A[i][k] * B[k][j]
```

This is the **ideal** pattern for SIMD: independent accumulations across the innermost dimension, regular stride access, and no data dependencies across iterations (except the accumulator).

## Benchmark Measurements

### Test Code (`matrix_mult.cpp`)

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <chrono>
#include <random>
#include <iomanip>

// Simple naive matrix multiplication (N x N)
// Designed to be row-major, cache-friendly for C[i][j] += A[i][k] * B[k][j]
// We use flatten 1D arrays with explicit indexing to avoid vector overhead

void matmul_naive(const double* A, const double* B, double* C, int N) {
    // Clear C
    for (int i = 0; i < N * N; i++) {
        C[i] = 0.0;
    }

    // Standard i-j-k order (cache-friendly for A and C, poor for B)
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < N; j++) {
            double sum = 0.0;
            for (int k = 0; k < N; k++) {
                sum += A[i * N + k] * B[k * N + j];
            }
            C[i * N + j] = sum;
        }
    }
}

// Benchmark harness
double benchmark_matmul(int N, int warmup_runs, int measured_runs) {
    std::vector<double> A(N * N), B(N * N), C(N * N);

    // Initialize with random values to prevent compiler from optimizing away
    std::mt19937 rng(42);
    std::uniform_real_distribution<double> dist(0.1, 1.0);
    for (int i = 0; i < N * N; i++) {
        A[i] = dist(rng);
        B[i] = dist(rng);
    }

    // Warmup
    for (int r = 0; r < warmup_runs; r++) {
        matmul_naive(A.data(), B.data(), C.data(), N);
    }

    // Measured runs with flush to prevent caching tricks
    std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::time_point start, end;
    double total_time = 0.0;
    for (int r = 0; r < measured_runs; r++) {
        // Invalidate caches via memset to C (hacky but effective for local benchmark)
        std::memset(C.data(), 0, N * N * sizeof(double));

        start = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
        matmul_naive(A.data(), B.data(), C.data(), N);
        end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

        total_time += std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(end - start).count();
    }

    return total_time / measured_runs;  // average microseconds
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> sizes = {64, 128, 256, 512, 1024};
    int warmup = 5;
    int runs = 20;

    std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(1);
    std::cout << "N\tTime (µs) -O2\tTime (µs) -O3\tSpeedup\n";
    std::cout << "---\t-----------\t-----------\t-------\n";

    for (int N : sizes) {
        // These calls are conceptual; in practice you'd compile separately
        // See compilation instructions below
        double t_o2 = benchmark_matmul(N, warmup, runs);
        double t_o3 = benchmark_matmul(N, warmup, runs);
        double speedup = t_o2 / t_o3;
        std::cout << N << "\t" << t_o2 << "\t\t" << t_o3 << "\t\t" << speedup << "x\n";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

