## When the Difference *Doesn't* Matter

| Scenario | Recommendation |
|---|---|
| Collection < ~10K items | LINQ — readability wins |
| Background/off-CPU-bound task | LINQ — developer time > CPU time |
| One-off filter on startup | LINQ — negligible impact |
| Code path hit millions of times/sec | `for` loop — critical path optimization |
| Team values code clarity heavily | LINQ — prefer maintainability |

## When the Difference *Does* Matter

| Scenario | Recommendation |
|---|---|
| Hot path in a game server, trading system, or high-throughput API | `for` loop |
| 10M+ items filtered repeatedly in a batch job | `for` loop with pre-sized result |
| Memory-constrained environment | `for` loop (lower allocation pressure → less GC pressure) |
| Real-time processing with latency SLAs | `for` loop |

## Optimized `for` Loop Pattern

```csharp
public static List<int> FilterFast(List<int> source, int threshold)
{
    // Option 1: Pre-count if you want exact size (extra O(n) pass)
    int count = 0;
    foreach (var item in source)
    {
        if (item >= threshold) count++;
    }

    var result = new List<int>(count); // Exact capacity, zero reallocations
    foreach (var item in source)
    {
        if (item >= threshold)
            result.Add(item);
    }
    return result;

    // Option 2: Two-pass vs one-pass tradeoff.
    // For 10M items, a single pass with reasonable initial capacity is usually better:
}

public static List<int> FilterFastSinglePass(List<int> source, int threshold)
{
    // Estimate ~10% based on known distribution (tune this!)
    var result = new List<int>(source.Count / 10);
    for (int i = 0; i < source.Count; i++)
    {
        if (source[i] >= threshold)
            result.Add(source[i]);
    }
    return result;
}
```

## Advanced: Span-Based Filtering (Maximum Performance)

If you need absolute peak performance on .NET 8:

```csharp
using System.Runtime.CompilerServices;

public static int[] FilterSpan(List<int> source, int threshold)
{
    Span<int> sourceSpan = CollectionsMarshal.AsSpan(source);
    var result = new int[sourceSpan.Length]; // Worst case upper bound
    int count = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < sourceSpan.Length; i++)
    {
        if (sourceSpan[i] >= threshold)
        {
            result[count++] = sourceSpan[i];
        }
    }

    // Resize to exact size (or return count for use with a pre-allocated pool)
    var exactResult = new int[count];
    Array.Copy(result, exactResult, count);
    return exactResult;
}
```

This avoids `List<T>` overhead entirely and can be **2–3x faster** than even the optimized `for` loop with `List<T>`.

