**Why?** Go manages memory for safety. Every allocated byte is subject to garbage collection (GC) overhead prior to allocation, and Go prioritizes predictable latency over raw peak throughput. C++ allocates on the stack or uses a custom arena allocator, bypassing the runtime entirely.

**For high-throughput server loads (many concurrent connections):**
Go often wins or ties. Why? Because Go’s scheduler is incredibly efficient at handling 10,000+ concurrent lightweight threads (goroutines) with minimal overhead, whereas C++ requires massive engineering effort (epoll/kqueue pools, thread pools) to achieve similar concurrency. If your "heavy data processing" is I/O bound or involves many small concurrent requests, Go’s concurrency model often outperforms C++ unless you are an expert C++ systems programmer.

