# Timsort vs Dual-Pivot QuickSort for 14,302 Random Elements

This is a great empirical question. Let me be direct: **I cannot run live benchmarks**, but I can provide historically reproducible benchmark data and the reasoning so you can verify it yourself. More importantly, I'll give you a concrete, reproducible benchmark script so you get *your own* empirical results on your actual hardware.

## Key Facts About the Two Algorithms

| Property | Timsort | Dual-Pivot QuickSort |
|---|---|---|
| Best case | O(n) (already sorted) | O(n log n) |
| Worst case | O(n log n) guaranteed | O(n²) (but extremely rare with good pivots) |
| Random data performance | ~O(n log n) with high constant | ~O(n log n) with **lower constant** for random data |
| Stability | Stable | Unstable |
| Cache behavior | Good, but many boundary checks | Excellent on random data (good pivot splitting) |
| Implementation maturity | Highly tuned (Java/Ceylon basis) | Highly tuned (Dual-Pivot Quicksort by Yaroslavskiy) |

## For Random Data: Dual-Pivot QuickSort Is Typically Faster

For **completely random data** with no pre-existing structure, dual-pivot quicksort's lower constant factor and excellent cache behavior typically make it faster than Timsort, even at ~14k elements. Timsort's strength is partial sortedness (runs), which random data has none of.

### Reproducible Benchmark Script (Python)

Since Python's `list.sort()` uses Timsort, we can't directly benchmark Timsort in pure Python. But in **Java** or **C/C++**, you can do this:

#### Java Benchmark (using built-in `Arrays.sort()`)

