## Key Observations

### 1. The Crossover Point Exists
For **very small** initial sizes (< ~500 elements), `std::vector` can actually be competitive or even faster because:
- The O(n) memory moves happen in L1 cache → extremely fast
- `std::list` suffers per-node allocation overhead (malloc/free has ~20–50ns base cost on many systems)
- Pointer chasing in `list` causes cache misses that outweigh simple block moves

As size grows, vector's O(n) cost dominates, and **list pulls ahead significantly**.

### 2. Element Size Dramatically Affects the Gap
With larger elements, `memmove` becomes much more expensive for vector, widening the gap. For 128-byte elements, vector is **30–70x slower** at large N, versus **3–10x** for 32-byte elements.

### 3. `std::list` Isn't Free: The Hidden Costs
```
List node overhead per element:
├── next pointer      (8 bytes on 64-bit)
├── prev pointer      (8 bytes on 64-bit)
├── allocator bookkeeping (varies, often 0–16 bytes)
├── element data      (N bytes)
└── Heap fragmentation from many small allocations

Effective memory usage: ~24 bytes header + N bytes data + allocator overhead
```

- **Allocation/deallocation overhead**: Each `list` insertion calls `new`/`delete` (or the allocator's allocate/deallocate). On glibc's malloc, small allocations after many frees can trigger coalescing and bookkeeping overhead.
- **Cache thrashing**: Random middle insertions scatter nodes across the heap. Iterating or even locating the insertion point via `std::next` causes cache misses. This is why list time barely grows with size — the dominant cost is

# Regex for HTML Parsing: Efficiency and Correctness Concerns

## Short Answer

Your current regex has **significant problems** — both correctness and performance-wise. Here's why, along with better alternatives.

---

## Problems with Your Regex

### 1. Catastrophic Backtracking Risk ⚠️

Your pattern:
```perl
/<a\s+[^>]*href="([^"]*)"[^>]*>/g
```

The subpatterns `[^>]*` before and after the `href` attribute each match any character except `>`. In **malformed or adversarial HTML**, this can cause massive backtracking. For example:

```html
<a href="..."> Lots of text without > characters <a href="...">
```

While `[^>]*` is generally safe from *catastrophic* backtracking (it's a bounded negative class, not a nested quantifier), it still performs **linear scans** across potentially very long tag contents. On multi-GB files, this adds up.

### 2. Correctness Issues

- **Only matches `href="..."`** — Misses:
  - `href='...'` (single quotes)
  - `href=...` (no quotes)
  - `HREF="..."` (uppercase)
  - Whitespace variations (tabs, newlines)
- **Assumes `href` comes after other attributes** — Fails if `href` is the first attribute:
  ```html
  <a href="http://example.com" class="link">
  ```
  Your regex requires `<a\s+[^>]*` **before** `href`, so it needs at least one non-`>` char after `<a`. If `href` is immediately after `<a `, the `[^>]*` will match it, but the ordering is fragile.
- **Doesn't handle HTML entities** in attributes
- **Fails on malformed HTML** (missing `>`, nested `<` in attribute values, etc.)
- **Captures only the href**, but returns the full match list depending on context (you're storing into `@links`, so you get **all captured groups**, which is just the hrefs in this case — OK, but fragile)

### 3. Performance on Large Files

Even if it works, loading **entire multi-GB HTML dumps into memory** (`$html_content`) is a bottleneck. Regex must scan the whole string in memory.

---

## Better Approaches (Ranked)

### Option 1: Use a Proper HTML Parser (🏆 Best for Production)

```perl
use HTML::LinkExtor;
use IO::File;

my $extor = HTML::LinkExtor->new(\&link_callback);

sub link_callback {
    my ($tag, %attr) = @_;
    if ($tag eq 'a' && $attr{href}) {
        print "$attr{href}\n";
    }
}

# Stream-parse instead of loading entire file into memory
my $file = IO::File->new("huge_dump.html", "r") or die "Cannot open: $!";
while (my $line = <$file>) {
    $extor->parse($line);
}
$extor->eof();
$file->close();
```

**Why this is better:**
- **Streams input** → low memory footprint
- **Correctly parses HTML** → handles quotes, case, whitespace, entities, malformed markup
- **C++ backend** (`HTML::Parser`) → very fast
- **No regex → no backtracking concerns**

### Option 2: Optimized Regex (If You Must Use Regex)

If you absolutely must use regex (e.g., no CPAN modules allowed), fix the pattern:

```perl
# Case-insensitive, handles single/double/no quotes, any attribute order
my @links = $html_content =~ /<a\b(?=[^>]*\bhref\s*=\s*(?:"([^"]*)"|'([^']*)'|(\S+)))([^>]*)>/gi;

# Combine the three possible href captures
my @hrefs;
for (my $i = 0; $i < scalar(@links); $i += 4) {
    push @hrefs, defined $links[$i+1] ? $links[$i+1]
                  : defined $links[$i+2] ? $links[$i+2]
                  : $links[$i+3];
}
```

Or simpler, using a lookahead:

```perl
my @links = $html_content =~ /<a\b[^>]*\bhref\s*=\s*(?:"([^"]*)"|'([^']*)'|(\S+))[^>]*>/gi;

my @hrefs;
while (@links) {
    push @hrefs, shift(@links) // shift(@links) // shift(@links);
}
```

Even better — use ` Troposharp` atomic groups or possessive quantifiers to prevent backtracking:

```perl
# Perl 5.10+ with /a (ASCII) and atomic grouping
my @links = $html_content =~ /<a\b(?>[^>]*?)\bhref\s*=\s*(?:"(?>[^"]*)"|'(?>[^']*)'|(\S+))(?>[^>]*)>/gi;
```

### Option 3: Stream + Regex (Middle Ground)

```perl
use IO::File;

my $file = IO::File->new("huge_dump.html", "r") or die $!;

while (my $line = <$file>) {
    my @hrefs = $line =~ /<a\b[^>]*\bhref\s*=\s*["']?([^>"'\s]+)["']?[^>]*>/gi;
    print "$_\n" for @hrefs;
}
```

This avoids loading the entire file into memory. The regex is still imperfect but far less risky on a per-line basis.

---

## Benchmark Comparison

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use Benchmark qw(cmpthese);

# Generate a 10MB HTML string for testing
my $html = join '', map {
    "<a href=\"http://example.com/$_" class=\"link$_" onclick=\"doSomething()\">Link $_</a>"
} 1..50000;

# Add some noise to make it realistic
$html .= "\n<div>Noise: $html</div>\n" x 100;

my %results;

# Method 1: Original regex
sub original_regex {
