## 1. C++ Implementation (Raw Power & Control)

We use **simdjson**, a state-of-the-art C++ library that uses SIMD instructions (AVX2/AVX-512) to parse JSON at memory bandwidth limits. It is currently the fastest general-purpose JSON parser in the world.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <simdjson.h>

// Flat storage for extracted data to avoid object overhead
struct ValidationResult {
    std::vector<int64_t> ids;
    std::vector<size_t> name_lengths;
};

Result parse_json_simd(const std::u8string& json, ValidationResult& result) {
    ondemand::parser parser;
    auto doc = parser.iterate(json);

    // Navigate to sequence
    ondemand::object root = doc.get_object();
    for (auto sequence : root) {
        // The JSON structure is {"users": [ {...}, {...} ]}
        // We assume the key is "users"

        // Reset parser for the next iteration if needed, but iterate handles it
    }

    // Simpler approach: iterate directly if root is array
    // Assuming input is [{"id": 1, "name": "Alice"}, ...]
    for (auto raw : doc) {
        auto obj = raw.get_object();
        for (auto item : obj) {
            if (item.unescaped_key().value_view() == u8"id") {
                result.ids.push_back(item.get_value().get_int64());
            } else if (item.unescaped_key().value_view() == u8"name") {
                auto name_val = item.get_value().get_cstring();
                result.name_lengths.push_back(name_val.value().size());
            }
        }
    }

    return SUCCESS;
}

int main() {
    // Load 100MB JSON file
    std::ifstream file("large.json", std::ios::binary);
    auto json_data = std::string(std::istreambuf_iterator<char>(file), {});

    ValidationResult result;
    auto start = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

    auto error = parse_json_simd(json_data, result);
    if (error) {
        std::cerr << simdjson::error_message(error) << "\n";
        return 1;
    }

    auto end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli> elapsed = end - start;

    std::cout << "C++ simdjson: " << elapsed.count() << " ms\n";
    std::cout << "Parsed IDs: " << result.ids.size() << "\n";

    return 0;
}
```

**Key Optimizations:**
*   **simdjson:** Uses SIMD instructions to process multiple JSON tokens in parallel.
*   **Zero-Copy Parsing:** Does not allocate new string objects for keys; it points directly into the input buffer.
*   **Flat Vectors:** Avoids `std::map` or `std::unordered_map` overhead.

