# Why `std::vector` Wins Despite O(n) Complexity

| Factor | `std::vector` | `std::list` |
|---|---|---|
| **Insert complexity** | O(n) element moves | O(1) pointer update |
| **Memory layout** | Contiguous | Scattered heap nodes |
| **Cache behavior** | **Sequential reads/writes → high cache hit rate** | Random pointer chasing → **cache thrashing** |
| **Allocation cost** | None per insert (pre-allocated) | **One heap allocation per insert** |
| **Hardware optimization** | `memmove` uses SIMD/streaming stores | No bulk operation benefit |
| **Branch prediction** | Predictable loop for `memmove` | Unpredictable node traversal |

## The Cache Miss Story

Each `std::list` node contains at least two pointers (`prev`, `next`) plus the data. For an `int` element, each node is **24 bytes** (on 64-bit). These nodes are scattered across the heap. When you insert at position `pos`:

