# Regex Performance for HTML Parsing in Perl

## The Short Answer

Your regex is **not ideal for production**, but probably **not catastrophically slow** for this specific pattern. However, the bigger concern is **correctness**, not just performance.

## Why Regex for HTML Is Problematic

```perl
# Your regex works for well-formed, simple cases:
# <a href="..." >
# <a class="x" href="..." id="y">
```

But it will **fail silently** on common real-world cases:

```html
<!-- Single quotes -->
<a href='http://example.com'>Link</a>

<!-- No quotes -->
<a href=http://example.com>Link</a>

<!-- Newlines in attributes -->
<a
   href="http://example.com"
   class="link">Link</a>

<!-- Nested quotes -->
<a href="http://example.com" data-title="It's a link">Link</a>

<!-- href not the first attribute -->
<a class="btn" href="http://example.com">Link</a>
<center><!-- your regex might still work, but order matters? -->

<!-- Malformed / unclosed tags -->
<a href="http://example.com">
```

## Performance Analysis

Let's benchmark your approach against alternatives:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use Benchmark qw(cmpthese);

# Simulate a realistic HTML snippet (100KB+)
my $html = join '', map {
    "<div><p><a class='nav-link' href='/page/$_' title='Page $_'>Link $_</a>"
    . " <a href=\"/other/$_\" id='link$_'>Other $_</a></p></div>\n"
} 1..5000;

# Method 1: Your original regex
my $re1 = qr/<a\s+[^>]*href="([^"]*)"[^>]*>/g;

# Method 2: More robust regex (handles single quotes, unordered attributes)
my $re2 = qr/<a\s+(?:[^>]*?\s+)?href=(?:"([^"]*)"|'([^']*)'|(\S+))[^>]*>/gi;

# Method 3: HTML::TreeBuilder (proper parser)
use HTML::TreeBuilder 5 -strict;

sub parse_with_regex1 {
    my @links;
    while ($html =~ /<a\s+[^>]*href="([^"]*)"[^>]*>/g) {
        push @links, $1;
    }
    return @links;
}

sub parse_with_regex2 {
    my @links;
    while ($html =~ /<a\s+(?:[^>]*?\s+)?href=(?:"([^"]*)"|'([^']*)'|(\S+))[^>]*>/gi) {
        push @links, defined $1 ? $1 : defined $2 ? $2 : $3;
    }
    return @links;
}

sub parse_with_treebuilder {
    my $tree = HTML::TreeBuilder->new_from_content($html);
    my @links = map { $_->attr('href') } $tree->look_down(_tag => 'a');
    $tree->delete;  # Important: free memory
    return @links;
}

sub parse_with_re {
    my @links;
    $html =~ /href=["']?([^"'\s>]+)["']?/g;
    return @links;
}

cmpthese(-5, {
    regex1        => \&parse_with_regex1,
    regex2        => \&parse_with_regex2,
    treebuilder   => \&parse_with_treebuilder,
    simple_re     => \&parse_with_re,
});
```

### Typical Results

| Method | Speed | Correctness | Memory |
|---|---|---|---|
| `regex1` (yours) | ⚡ Fast | ❌ Fragile | Low |
| `regex2` (robust RE) | ⚡ Fast | ⚠️ Better but still fragile | Low |
| `simple_re` (grep href) | ⚡ Fastest | ❌ Catch-all (picks up `href` in comments, strings) | Low |
| `HTML::TreeBuilder` | 🐢 10-50x slower | ✅ Correct | High |

## Key Insight: `[^>]*` Is Your Biggest Risk

```regex
# Your pattern:
<a\s+[^>]*href="([^"]*)"[^>]*>

# The [^>]* before href is greedy and can be problematic.
# Consider this pathological case:
<a <a <a <a ... <a href="real">  # deeply nested "<" without ">"
# Worst case: O(n^2) if there are many '<' characters before the real '>'
```

However, since `[^>]*` **cannot backtrack over `>`**, it's actually **linear** in practice — different from true catastrophic backtracking. The real risk is:

1. **Very long attributes** with many `<` characters (unlikely in valid HTML)
2. **Misleading performance**: fast but wrong results

## Recommended Approaches by Use Case

### If correctness matters (production):

```perl
use HTML::TreeBuilder 5 -strict;

sub extract_links_from_html {
    my ($html) = @_;
    my $tree = HTML::TreeBuilder->new_from_content($html);
    my @links = map {
        my $href = $_->attr('href');
        next unless defined $href;
        # Resolve relative URLs if needed
        $href;
    } $tree->look_down(_tag => 'a');
    $tree->delete;  # Critical: prevents memory leak
    return @links;
}
```

### If performance is critical and HTML is well-formed:

```perl
use HTML::LinkExtor;

my $extor = HTML::LinkExtor->new(\&collect_links, '');
$extor->parse($html_content);
$extor->eof;

sub collect_links {
    my ($tag, %attr) = @_;
    return unless $tag eq 'a';
    print $attr{href} . "\n" if $attr{href};
}
```

`HTML::LinkExtor` is **significantly faster** than `HTML::TreeBuilder` because it uses a SAX-like stream parser — no DOM tree is built.

### Stream-processing for multi-GB files:

```perl
use HTML::TokeParser;

my $p = HTML::TokeParser->new(\$html_content)
    or die "Failed to parse: $!";

while (my $token = $p->get_token) {
    next unless $token->[0] eq 'S';  # Start tag
    next unless $token->[1] eq 'a';
    my ($href) = @{$token->[3]}{'href'};
    print "$href\n" if $href;
}
```

## Memory Considerations for Large Files

```perl
# DON'T: Load entire file into memory
my $html = do { local $/; <FILE> };  # GBs of RAM!

# DO: Process in chunks or stream
use HTML::Stream;
my $stream = HTML::Stream->new(file => 'large_dump.html');
while (my $element = $stream->get_element) {
    next unless $element->tag eq 'a';
    print $element->get_attr('href'), "\n";
}
```

