auto it = find_nearby_position(some_iterator, value);  // O(k) for local search, not O(n)
container.insert(it, value);  // O(1) for list, O(remaining_elements) for vector
```

---

## Recommended Alternatives for This Use Case

If you真的很 need frequent middle insertions on very large collections, consider:

| Alternative | When to Use |
|---|---|
| **`std::deque`** | Better than vector for middle insertions in some cases; blocks of contiguous memory reduce some overhead |
| **`boost::container::flat_list`** | Sorted flat structure with O(log n) insert if sorted |
| **Skip list** | O(log n) insert, good cache behavior |
| **B-tree / Ebtree** | O(log n) insert, excellent for disk-backed or very large in-memory sets |
| **`std::vector` + hybrid approach** | If insertions are clustered (e.g., always near beginning), consider a deque-like structure (two vectors, one for front inserts) |
| **Gap buffer** | Ideal for text-editing workloads; maintains a "gap" that shifts with edits |

