# Using Regex for HTML Parsing: A Perl Performance Analysis

## The Short Answer

**No, this is not the recommended approach for production**, and here's why — but not primarily because of catastrophic backtracking.

## Why Your Regex Is *Actually* Safe from Catastrophic Backtracking

Your regex:
```perl
/<a\s+[^>]*href="([^"]*)"[^>]*>/g
```

This is **linear-time** and **will not catastrophically backtrack** because:
- `[^>]*` is a *bounded* quantifier (it stops at `>`)
- `[^"]*` is a *bounded* quantifier (it stops at `"`)
- There is **no nested quantification** or ambiguous alternative paths that cause exponential backtracking

So catastrophic backtracking is **not your concern here**. Your real concerns are different.

## Real Problems with This Approach

### 1. **It Fragments on Valid HTML Variations**
Your regex misses many legitimate cases:
```html
<a HREF="http://example.com">          <!-- uppercase -->
<a href='http://example.com'>          <!-- single quotes -->
<a href=http://example.com>            <!-- no quotes -->
<a href="http://example.com" title="test">  <!-- href not first attribute -->
<a class="foo" href="http://example.com">   <!-- attributes before href -->
<a\n   href="http://example.com">         <!-- multiline/whitespace -->
```

Your regex assumes:
- `href` uses double quotes only
- `href` appears **after** `<a` and **before** the closing `>`
- No whitespace quirks

### 2. **It Doesn't Handle Nested/Complex Structures**
If the HTML is malformed or contains `<a>` inside other tags, your regex may produce false positives.

### 3. **Performance Is Still Worse Than DOM Parsing for Large Files**

